
==========================================================================
(1) IS mu_0 SCALE-INVARIANT?  mu_0(cE) vs mu_0(E)
==========================================================================
  E=[0, 1, 2]
    c= 1: mu_0(cE)=0.500000=1/2
    c= 2: mu_0(cE)=0.500000=1/2
    c= 3: mu_0(cE)=0.500000=1/2
    c= 5: mu_0(cE)=0.500000=1/2
    c= 7: mu_0(cE)=0.500000=1/2
    c=10: mu_0(cE)=0.500000=1/2
  E=[0, 1, 2, 3]
    c= 1: mu_0(cE)=0.333333=1/3
    c= 2: mu_0(cE)=0.333333=1/3
    c= 3: mu_0(cE)=0.333333=1/3
    c= 5: mu_0(cE)=0.333333=1/3
    c= 7: mu_0(cE)=0.333333=1/3
    c=10: mu_0(cE)=0.333333=1/3
  E=[0, 1, 3]
    c= 1: mu_0(cE)=0.476190=10/21
    c= 2: mu_0(cE)=0.476190=10/21
    c= 3: mu_0(cE)=0.476190=10/21
    c= 5: mu_0(cE)=0.476190=10/21
    c= 7: mu_0(cE)=0.476190=10/21
    c=10: mu_0(cE)=0.476190=10/21
  CONCLUSION: mu_0 with FIXED arc is NOT scale-invariant (unlike mu).
  => mu_0(AP scaled) can differ. The 0-anchor breaks scaling symmetry.

